// Notes and scales
// note() plays pitches: a letter from
// a to g, maybe # or b for sharp or
// flat, and an octave: c3, eb4, f#2.
// A higher number is a higher octave.
//
// Try: change the notes. Then swap
// triangle for sawtooth, square or
// sine: the four basic synths.

setcpm(30)
$: note("c3 e3 g3 c4")
  .s("triangle")
// ---
// Notes as numbers
// note() takes numbers too: 60 is
// c4, 61 is a half step higher, 72
// is an octave higher.
//
// Try: write a tune with numbers.

setcpm(30)
$: note("60 62 64 67")
  .s("triangle")
// ---
// Scales
// With n() and .scale(), numbers are
// steps of a scale: 0 is the root, 1
// the next note of the scale, and so
// on. No note can be wrong.
//
// Try: C:major, D:dorian, E:phrygian.
// The Reference lists every scale.

setcpm(30)
$: n("0 2 4 <3 5> 4 2 0 ~")
  .scale("C:minor")
  .s("triangle")
// ---
// Sampled instruments
// Recorded instruments play notes
// too: piano, and the gm_ sounds of
// the Sounds panel's Instruments.
//
// Try: swap piano for gm_epiano1 or
// gm_acoustic_bass. Change C3 into
// C2 or C4 to move the octave.

setcpm(30)
$: n("0 [2 4] <7 6> 4")
  .scale("C3:minor")
  .s("piano")
// ---
// Chords
// Notes joined by commas in [ ] make
// a chord; < > plays one chord per
// cycle.
//
// Try: add chords, like [bb2,d3,f3]
// and [g2,b2,d3]. Change one note
// and hear the color change.

setcpm(30)
$: note("<[c3,eb3,g3] [ab2,c3,eb3]>")
  .s("piano")
$: note("<c2 ab1>").s("sawtooth")
  .lpf(500)
